            "n_events": 42,
            "result_text": "## Setup Steps\n\n**1. Initialize the git server:**\n```bash\nbash /tmp/setup-git-server.sh\n```\n\n**2. Start the web server** (in a separate terminal or background):\n```bash\nbash /tmp/start-webserver.sh\n# Listens on http://localhost:8080\n```\n\n## Usage Workflow\n\nNow you can:\n\n```bash\n# Clone the repository\ngit clone user@server:/git/server my-project\ncd my-project\n\n# Make changes\necho \"hello world\" > hello.html\ngit add hello.html\ngit commit -m \"add hello.html\"\ngit push origin master\n```\n\n**Verify it works:**\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:8080/hello.html\n# Output: hello world\n```\n\n## How It Works\n\n- When you `git push`, the bare repository receives your changes\n- The `post-receive` hook automatically runs and checks out files to `/var/www/html`\n- The web server serves those files on port 8080\n\n## Optional: Use nginx instead of Python server\n\nIf you prefer nginx (more production-like):\n```bash\n# Install nginx\nsudo apt-get install nginx\n\n# Configure /etc/nginx/sites-available/default\n# Set root /var/www/html;\n\n# Start nginx\nsudo systemctl start nginx\n```\n\nThe Python http.server works fine for local testing. Just run the start-webserver.sh script and you're good to go!"
